ve existed at  all and if you can really think about  that that's why depression is so hard to  get out of that's why when someone says  just cheer up it doesn't make any sense  in that kind of situation mental  is not a choice well I actually don't  think I wanted to be happy at that  period of time because I didn't want  anything even if I did it's not like I  could make that happen because this fog  just keeps coming in and in and in so  the way that I actually got through it  was kind of lucky I was still living at  home I was really young and I had  parents who believed me into doing stuff  they got sick of me living this  non-existent life and just straight up  made me get a job I think that mine aliy  helped but the thing that helped me move  in anything was exercised I think this  is another thing that my mom actually  specifically made me do and that helped  me so so much and without my parents and  that position of authority telling me to  do something I mean not feeling like I  could say no because I didn't even have  to fight in me to rebel against anything  I don't know how I would have gotten out  of it at all so it went away and then  sometimes came back but uh one of the  most recent experiences I had where it  really affected me was at the Sun of  last year I was in the stage of culture  shock where I was super sick I got sick  and ultimately got pneumonia so in the  period where I was already really sad  being away from my family who I am  incredibly close to and the one thing  that was kind of distracting me was that  I got to go to work and welcome cool  stuff and meet new people and get to  know the people in my office but then I  got pneumonia and I had to take a full  two weeks off of work at the worst  possible time and it almost killed me it  was really awful and really unfortunate  and I was on codeine I wasn't allowed to  leave the house I couldn't drive I  couldn't do very much at all I didn't  really have an appetite so it was like  full depression almost in that same time  though the witness came out and I truly  believe the witness the video game saves  me from falling back into crippling  depression for anyone doesn't know it is  a challenging puzzle game you walk  around these beautiful colorful  environments and solve puzzles that  teach you how to solve other puzzles in  the same game sort of like it has its  own language so it was something that  was teaching me constantly and I was  learning constantly and most importantly  I felt like I was still accomplishing  things and had  goals to strive towards which is  something that I think video games do  better than pretty much any other medium  the witness being a puzzle game is a  perfect example but they've definitely  been other times in my life where video  games and the sense of accomplishment  you get from them even from completing a  mundane side quest in an MMO you still  feel a spark and you don't have to be  totally able-bodied and you don't have  to go anywhere and it's almost like a  passive way to elevate your mental  health or at least that's how it works  for me and I really believe there are  terrible times that video games have  helped me with more than anything else  more than human interaction just more  than anything so I wanted to tell you  guys about this Kickstarter this is my  mad I wasn't asked to make this video  this is just something that I really do  care about it's called the checkpoint  series and it's basically a webseries  it's raising awareness about mental  health issues and helping those affected  using the power of video games so talked  about how mental health issues are the  single biggest cause of disability in  young people and the single biggest  cause of death of people under 30 years  old through thoughts of those people  know to get help because it's  stigmatized and they feel bad about it  because it's too expensive all because  they don't know they have the problem  and I definitely feel like I personally  sat in that category and it sucked but  they also want to help people to  recognize common problems in other  people and also in video games so  something the series is going to explore  that I'm really interested in is how  mental health is represented in video  games specifically and how we can use  games to learn more about these kinds of  things so I mean you guys can read the  whole thing on Kickstarter I'll leave a  link below but it's something that I  think is awesome  and I guess that is pretty much it for  this video anyone else has similar  stories I would absolutely love to hear  mental health is something that we  should take just as seriously as  physical health and that is it for me  today you guys I will see y'all next  time  bye
 

